                "text": "I'm noticing each quadrant has one colored cell and the pattern fills them like a latin square, deriving the remaining values by tracking which numbers pair with which positions across the four quadrants.\n\nComparing outputs, TL seems to serve as the base pattern, and TR, BL, BR each look like reflections or rotations of it - TR appears rotated 180\u00b0, BL mirrored horizontally, BR mirrored vertically.  Checking the full grid for 180\u00b0 symmetry doesn't quite work though, so I'm reconsidering whether the relationship between quadrants is something other than simple reflection.\n\nActually the pattern holds: TR=180(TL), BL=flipH(TL), BR=flipV(TL), confirmed by matching training positions consistently. Now applying this to the test grid, I extract TL, TR, BL, BR quadrant values and map each back to determine TL: it comes out to [[1,8],[7,3]], then I derive TR as the 180\u00b0 rotation, giving [[3,7],[8,1]].\n\nContinuing to fill in BL as the horizontal flip and BR as the vertical flip, I assemble the full grid row by row with the middle row and column staying as the fixed value 5, and get the complete five-row output.",
